course: dictionary
lesson: 6056
title: Words on the menu
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A menu is a list of the food and drinks you can choose in a café or restaurant. A menu can also mean the food planned for a meal or a day. «The kids' menu had fish fingers and ice cream.» The words kids' tell you this list is for children.
explain: A menu is not only on paper. On a computer or phone, a menu is a list of choices on the screen, like «the main menu», where you click the option you want. Look at the words around menu to work out which kind it is: food, or a screen?
explain: Menus are real texts you can read every day. «Spelling can be practised anywhere: signs, menus, number plates.» If you do not know a dish, read the words around it, such as the price and the other food on the list, and use them as clues.
fact: A menu is a list of choices, such as food in a café or buttons on a screen.
fact: «Menu» can mean the food planned for a meal, such as «a set menu» with soup, then chicken, then pudding.
fact: On a computer, a «drop-down menu» is a list that opens when you click.
fact: Menus often show prices, so people use them in maths to add costs and work out change.
fact: AI can read menus aloud, which helps people who find reading hard.
exercise: In a café, what is a menu? | A bill you pay at the end | A cup for hot drinks | *A list of the food and drinks you can choose
exercise: «The kids' menu had fish fingers and ice cream.» Who is the menu for? | Nobody | The cooks | Adults only | *Children
exercise: On a phone, «click the main menu» means what? | *A list of choices on the screen | A small plate of food | A map of the town | A kind of sound
test: «Our set menu is soup, chicken and pudding.» What does menu mean here? | A list of choices on a screen | *The food planned for the meal | A ticket for a train | A type of plate
test: On a computer, a drop-down menu does what? | Makes the sound louder | *Opens a list of choices when you click | Puts a picture on the screen | Deletes all your files
exam: Why are menus good for spelling practice, as in «Spelling can be practised anywhere: signs, menus, number plates»? | Menus never have words | The words are short and easy to forget | *The words are real and you can read and check them | You only need a pencil to read them
